WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.352
wind gusts blowing 40 miles per

00:00:02.485 --> 00:00:04.254
hour or more right now, raining

00:00:04.387 --> 00:00:04.587
heavily.

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:06.122
They could get 6 to 10 inches of

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:07.057
rain here.

00:00:07.057 --> 00:00:09.225
But the area of major concern,

00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:11.061
even as this hurricane moves

00:00:11.161 --> 00:00:12.729
inland toward Georgia, still as

00:00:13.063 --> 00:00:15.098
a hurricane, the major areas of

00:00:15.231 --> 00:00:17.067
concern are allowing the Florida

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.034
gulf coast from Tampa all the

00:00:18.168 --> 00:00:19.869
way up through the big bend

00:00:20.003 --> 00:00:22.038
where they're experiencing

00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:24.607
historic and devastating storm

00:00:24.741 --> 00:00:25.075
surge.

00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:27.710
I want to go right to bill weir

00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:31.081
in steinhatchee in Florida.

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.083
He's about an hour and a half

00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:35.785
from where I am now, but it's a

00:00:35.919 --> 00:00:38.521
world away in terms of

00:00:38.655 --> 00:00:39.089
devastation.

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:40.757
Bill, just describe what you're

00:00:41.091 --> 00:00:41.224
seeing.

00:00:41.224 --> 00:00:42.025
&gt;&gt; Reporter: John, the good news

00:00:42.158 --> 00:00:43.827
is, it seems like the surge has

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.795
crested here in steinhatchee.

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:47.497
You can see this line of debris,

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.532
there's some boxes and logs

00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:49.833
here.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:52.168
It has been receding ever since.

00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:53.736
But the devastation, it is

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.573
leaving behind is obvious.

00:00:56.706 --> 00:00:58.475
This is the dockside grill here.

00:00:58.608 --> 00:01:01.177
The steinhatchee river rushed

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:03.880
its banks.

00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:05.381
We knew that was going to

00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:05.715
happen.

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:07.150
It normally runs from east to

00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:07.484
west.

00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:08.318
We're looking south right now.

00:01:08.451 --> 00:01:11.921
The force of the dirty side of

00:01:12.055 --> 00:01:12.622
the storm has been forcing so

00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:15.125
much water upstream against the

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:16.426
current here and the victims of

00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:19.729
that are all of these fishing

00:01:19.863 --> 00:01:20.363
community villages, condo

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:20.763
communities.

00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:23.133
There's not a lot of development

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.134
around the corner this way.

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.768
Most of the town is behind us on

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:26.603
higher ground.

00:01:26.736 --> 00:01:27.837
So that's good news.

00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:30.507
But where you were yesterday,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.575
John, the marina, all of that we

00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:33.943
fear might have been wiped out

00:01:34.077 --> 00:01:36.679
as it's the first bend in the

00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:38.114
river coming off of the gulf of

00:01:38.248 --> 00:01:39.215
Mexico right here.

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:41.618
The family, the couple who owns

00:01:41.751 --> 00:01:43.753
this marina, you can see their

00:01:43.887 --> 00:01:45.622
mood shift from hopeful early in

00:01:45.755 --> 00:01:47.757
the morning to more resigned as

00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:49.192
the destruction becomes obvious

00:01:49.325 --> 00:01:49.492
here.

00:01:49.626 --> 00:01:51.161
But we're also starting to see

00:01:51.161 --> 00:01:52.228
people drive around which is

00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:55.365
probably not the wisest move

00:01:55.498 --> 00:01:55.732
right now.

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:56.432
There are still gust that is

00:01:56.566 --> 00:01:57.167
kicking up.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.435
Not as bad as we saw a few hours

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:01.171
ago, but you can understand

00:02:01.237 --> 00:02:01.971
people's instinct to want to

00:02:02.105 --> 00:02:03.907
check out the damage, see what

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:04.641
survived, what didn't.

00:02:04.774 --> 00:02:05.675
Too soon for that to say the

00:02:05.808 --> 00:02:05.975
least.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:09.712
John?

00:02:09.846 --> 00:02:11.181
&gt;&gt; Reporter: All right.

00:02:11.181 --> 00:02:11.915
Bill weir.

00:02:12.048 --> 00:02:13.917
At the top, just to be clear,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.919
you said for now, at least, the

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:17.921
surge seems to be going the

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:18.588
other direction?

00:02:18.721 --> 00:02:20.156
I know the tide could still come

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:20.857
in more.

00:02:21.191 --> 00:02:22.492
But for now, the worst seems to

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:27.830
have passed?

00:02:27.964 --> 00:02:28.598
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:31.367
You can see -- it's a very clear

00:02:31.501 --> 00:02:33.570
line where the high watermark

00:02:33.703 --> 00:02:35.905
left its debris there and it has

00:02:36.039 --> 00:02:38.508
been receding now fairly

00:02:38.641 --> 00:02:38.875
steadily.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:41.211
About as fast as it came up.

00:02:41.211 --> 00:02:42.812
At what point it will level off,

00:02:43.213 --> 00:02:43.513
we don't know.

00:02:43.646 --> 00:02:44.214
But the wind is still holding

00:02:44.347 --> 00:02:47.850
this water here.

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:48.451
&gt;&gt; Reporter: All right.

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:50.153
The wind is holding the water

00:02:50.286 --> 00:02:50.453
there.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:51.754
I'm sorry to hear about the

00:02:51.888 --> 00:02:55.225
family that owns the marina, it

00:02:55.325 --> 00:02:56.192
may be gone.

00:02:56.326 --> 00:02:57.327
Although, it is heartening to

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.430
know that they themselves are

00:03:00.563 --> 00:03:00.730
okay.

00:03:00.863 --> 00:03:02.365
Bill, every once in awhile we

00:03:02.498 --> 00:03:03.466
get this really pounding rain.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.200
What are you getting in terms of

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:08.104
rain there?

00:03:08.238 --> 00:03:09.939
&gt;&gt; Reporter: We're getting these

00:03:10.073 --> 00:03:11.241
bands now.

00:03:11.241 --> 00:03:13.643
We saw the 80, 90-mile-per-hour

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:14.644
gusts for a few minutes a few

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:15.511
hours ago.

00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:18.581
Now it's probably in the

00:03:18.715 --> 00:03:19.449
30-miles-per-hour range.

00:03:19.582 --> 00:03:20.717
Still dangerous.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.285
We're taking shelter behind one

00:03:22.418 --> 00:03:23.753
of these grand trees that are

00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:24.120
here.

00:03:24.254 --> 00:03:28.358
But we've seen plenty of this

00:03:28.491 --> 00:03:30.059
corrugated steel sheeting, tin

00:03:30.193 --> 00:03:32.328
roofs blowing around, peeling

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:33.263
off here.

00:03:33.329 --> 00:03:35.632
But, again, just to sort of

00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:37.467
level what the locals were

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.901
telling us, last night we went

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:40.737
out on a short boat cruise in

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.506
the calm before the storm with a

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.275
local captain here who said he

00:03:45.275 --> 00:03:47.343
was worried that the surge would

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:50.013
reach the roof line of the

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:51.281
dockside grill right there.

00:03:51.314 --> 00:03:54.550
It came up a few feet inside,

00:03:54.684 --> 00:03:57.287
but nowhere near their worst

00:03:57.287 --> 00:03:57.387
fears.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:58.521
That gives you some perspective

00:03:58.655 --> 00:03:59.622
that probably could have been a

00:03:59.756 --> 00:04:01.424
lot worse in this area.

00:04:01.557 --> 00:04:02.358
As far as what's happening

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:03.726
further up, Keaton beach, all of

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:04.661
that, we don't know yet.

00:04:04.794 --> 00:04:07.497
It's too soon to tell.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.532
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:10.767
We don't have eyes on some of

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.235
these other areas where the

00:04:12.368 --> 00:04:13.803
storm surge could be worse.

00:04:13.936 --> 00:04:15.638
Thank you so much for that

00:04:15.772 --> 00:04:16.039
perspective.

00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:18.541
Please stay safe and please keep

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:19.309
us posted over the next several

00:04:19.409 --> 00:04:22.378
hours as well.

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:23.980
Bill describing what he's seeing

00:04:24.113 --> 00:04:26.649
in steinhatchee there, a town of

00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:28.918
500 to 1,000 people depending on

00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:29.886
the time of the year.

00:04:30.019 --> 00:04:31.587
What they're going through which

00:04:31.721 --> 00:04:34.090
is quite bad and still

00:04:34.223 --> 00:04:35.325
developing right now, let's go

00:04:35.325 --> 00:04:36.893
now down to Tampa.

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:38.528
Obviously, one of the major

00:04:38.661 --> 00:04:39.429
metropolitan centers of the

00:04:39.562 --> 00:04:40.897
state of Florida where while the

00:04:41.331 --> 00:04:43.966
storm did not make a direct hit

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.436
there, not at all the storm

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:48.871
surge there has been a serious

00:04:49.005 --> 00:04:49.339
issue.

00:04:49.339 --> 00:04:52.108
Derek van dam is there.

00:04:52.241 --> 00:04:54.010
What are you seeing?

00:04:54.143 --> 00:04:56.012
&gt;&gt; The wind continues to push up

00:04:56.145 --> 00:04:57.480
the water from Tampa bay as

00:04:57.613 --> 00:04:58.681
well, John.

00:04:58.815 --> 00:05:00.650
We are getting now these

00:05:00.783 --> 00:05:02.185
intermittent tropical feeder

00:05:02.318 --> 00:05:06.322
bands and what was completely

00:05:06.456 --> 00:05:12.795
visible across Tampa bay has now

00:05:12.929 --> 00:05:13.529
been obscured by heavy sheets of

00:05:13.663 --> 00:05:13.996
rain coming in.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.598
It's raining at quite a clip.

00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:17.800
Probably a good half an inch to

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.369
an inch per hour.

00:05:19.369 --> 00:05:21.904
This will cause localized inland

00:05:22.038 --> 00:05:22.271
flooding.

00:05:22.405 --> 00:05:24.540
This is all working together to

00:05:24.674 --> 00:05:26.609
create the very difficult storm

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:30.146
surge conditions that you see

00:05:30.279 --> 00:05:33.383
unfolding behind us on bay shore

00:05:33.383 --> 00:05:33.449
boulevard.

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:35.017
This area floods during rainy

00:05:35.385 --> 00:05:35.551
days.

00:05:35.685 --> 00:05:35.952
It happens.

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:37.854
But this is in conjunction with

00:05:37.987 --> 00:05:39.455
the surge of water that has been

00:05:39.589 --> 00:05:41.391
pushed up with this major

00:05:41.391 --> 00:05:41.524
hurricane.

00:05:41.657 --> 00:05:44.260
The inland flooding meeting it

00:05:44.394 --> 00:05:45.395
at the same time.

00:05:45.461 --> 00:05:46.963
And then also the exaggerated

00:05:47.397 --> 00:05:49.732
tides from the supermoon and the

00:05:49.866 --> 00:05:51.501
full moon that's happening as we

00:05:51.634 --> 00:05:51.801
speak.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:55.037
We just went through the low

00:05:55.405 --> 00:05:55.438
tide cycle.

00:05:55.571 --> 00:05:56.439
That was at 7:56 this morning.

00:05:56.572 --> 00:05:57.940
We're working our way back up

00:05:58.074 --> 00:05:58.875
into high tide.

00:05:59.008 --> 00:06:02.412
So the concern here is that the

00:06:02.545 --> 00:06:03.413
surge that already broke record

00:06:03.513 --> 00:06:08.551
levels -- one of those gusts

00:06:08.684 --> 00:06:09.852
that come through.

00:06:09.986 --> 00:06:14.424
We're concerned that the surge

00:06:14.557 --> 00:06:19.629
later this afternoon, roughly

00:06:19.762 --> 00:06:20.296
2:00 P.M., are going to be

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:21.030
exacerbated by all of these kind

00:06:21.431 --> 00:06:23.900
of -- a company of issues here,

00:06:24.033 --> 00:06:27.537
the supermoon, the storm surge,

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:28.905
the push of the water from the

00:06:29.038 --> 00:06:30.606
storm and that's going to work

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.775
together to create even further

00:06:32.909 --> 00:06:34.977
storm surge flooding concerns

00:06:35.111 --> 00:06:38.080
here along the Tampa bay region.

00:06:38.214 --> 00:06:39.715
Tampa can't see it, but it is

00:06:39.849 --> 00:06:40.917
over my right shoulder.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:44.220
We've been here all day and the

00:06:44.353 --> 00:06:46.088
bay has just been angry.

00:06:46.222 --> 00:06:48.424
The water pushing up, creating

00:06:48.558 --> 00:06:52.228
waves, sometimes towering over

00:06:52.361 --> 00:06:53.596
two, three stories sometimes.

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:55.631
As these bands come in, they're

00:06:55.765 --> 00:06:57.467
stinging our face here.

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:58.901
There have been spectators from

00:06:59.035 --> 00:07:00.970
these houses that have come to

00:07:01.103 --> 00:07:03.473
see the scene unfolding and, you

00:07:03.573 --> 00:07:05.041
know, this is different for them

00:07:05.475 --> 00:07:06.709
because they know that this road

00:07:06.843 --> 00:07:07.477
can flood.

00:07:07.477 --> 00:07:09.479
But with this amount of water

00:07:09.545 --> 00:07:10.680
lapping up to the property

00:07:10.813 --> 00:07:12.014
lines, they know that their

00:07:12.148 --> 00:07:15.485
homes are at risk as well.

00:07:15.485 --> 00:07:16.486
John, so much going on here.

00:07:16.619 --> 00:07:19.489
I'm going to send it back to

00:07:19.489 --> 00:07:19.622
you.

00:07:19.622 --> 00:07:22.758
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Derek, if I can hang on

00:07:22.892 --> 00:07:24.727
to you for a second longer.

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:26.696
People are driving and they

00:07:26.829 --> 00:07:27.563
shouldn't be in this town.

00:07:27.697 --> 00:07:29.065
The rain is coming down really

00:07:29.499 --> 00:07:32.635
hard and I can tell it's coming

00:07:32.768 --> 00:07:36.672
down hard where you are now.

00:07:36.806 --> 00:07:40.209
But if I can ask you, lean onto

00:07:40.343 --> 00:07:41.511
your expertise for just a second

00:07:41.511 --> 00:07:42.345
for the storm surge, we were

00:07:42.478 --> 00:07:43.713
speaking to bill weir a moment

00:07:43.846 --> 00:07:45.815
ago in steinhatchee who said at

00:07:45.948 --> 00:07:47.984
least there it seems like the

00:07:48.117 --> 00:07:49.519
surge for now may have crested.

00:07:49.652 --> 00:07:51.521
They're seeing it back off a

00:07:51.521 --> 00:07:53.523
little bit and they're seeing a

00:07:53.523 --> 00:07:55.525
debris line there.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:56.325
Talk to us about how this could

00:07:56.459 --> 00:07:58.427
work over the next several hours

00:07:58.561 --> 00:08:02.365
up and down the big bend area,

00:08:02.498 --> 00:08:02.899
even where you are.

00:08:03.032 --> 00:08:04.000
Could you see the surge recede

00:08:04.133 --> 00:08:04.667
some and then come back in

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.672
again?

00:08:09.805 --> 00:08:10.907
&gt;&gt; Reporter: Okay, that's a

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:11.541
great question.

00:08:11.574 --> 00:08:13.743
And what we got to do, we got to

00:08:13.876 --> 00:08:17.713
visualize how a hurricane

00:08:17.847 --> 00:08:18.047
rotates.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.549
Counter clockwise, okay?

00:08:19.549 --> 00:08:21.551
And so I am on the eastern side

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:25.555
of where that center of where

00:08:25.555 --> 00:08:26.989
Idalia made landfall, where bill

00:08:27.123 --> 00:08:29.191
weir is located, I haven't

00:08:29.559 --> 00:08:31.093
checked a map, but I believe he

00:08:31.227 --> 00:08:32.628
would be on the backside of the

00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:32.929
storm.

00:08:33.062 --> 00:08:34.497
So the wind direction will be

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:35.698
completely opposite to what

00:08:35.831 --> 00:08:36.732
we're receiving here.

00:08:36.866 --> 00:08:38.134
And that's important.

00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:39.769
Let's compare it to Ian from

00:08:39.902 --> 00:08:43.205
September of last year.

00:08:43.573 --> 00:08:45.575
There is a major difference to

00:08:45.575 --> 00:08:46.809
what happened in Tampa bay that

00:08:46.943 --> 00:08:48.978
had the water sucked out because

00:08:49.111 --> 00:08:50.379
of the direction the wind was

00:08:50.513 --> 00:08:53.082
pushing offshore here in Tampa

00:08:53.215 --> 00:08:55.585
versus the onshore component

00:08:55.585 --> 00:08:57.720
that brought the surge to fort

00:08:57.853 --> 00:08:58.187
Myers beach.

00:08:58.321 --> 00:08:59.589
The same thing is happening

00:08:59.622 --> 00:08:59.789
here.

00:08:59.922 --> 00:09:01.223
We happen to be in the onshore

00:09:01.591 --> 00:09:02.792
component of this storm and that

00:09:02.925 --> 00:09:05.695
is why we continue to see this

00:09:05.828 --> 00:09:07.597
push, this surge of water that

00:09:07.597 --> 00:09:09.599
is coming directly off the gulf

00:09:09.732 --> 00:09:11.901
of Mexico.

00:09:12.034 --> 00:09:13.603
John?

00:09:13.603 --> 00:09:17.607
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a really good

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:18.040
explanation, Derek.

00:09:18.174 --> 00:09:18.741
If you can -- I can't see you.

00:09:18.874 --> 00:09:20.276
So I don't know if you're just

00:09:20.409 --> 00:09:21.944
getting poured on or blown

00:09:22.078 --> 00:09:22.745
around now or what.

00:09:22.878 --> 00:09:24.914
If you need to go, let me

00:09:25.047 --> 00:09:25.247
know --

00:09:25.615 --> 00:09:28.751
&gt;&gt; All of the above.

00:09:28.884 --> 00:09:29.652
&gt;&gt; This is such important

00:09:29.785 --> 00:09:30.419
information that we're both

00:09:30.553 --> 00:09:32.955
going to get wet for the time

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:33.623
being.

00:09:33.623 --> 00:09:34.890
In terms of the tides, you were

00:09:35.024 --> 00:09:36.726
talking about the supermoon and

